  "question": "Let $y = (x^2 + 1)^{\\tan^{-1} x}$. Show using logarithmic differentiation that $\\frac{dy}{dx} = (x^2 +1)^{\\tan^{-1} x}\\left(\\frac{2x \\tan^{-1} x}{x^2 + 1} + \\frac{1}{1 + x^2} \\ln(x^2 + 1)\\right)$",
  "answeroption": "C",
  "explanation": "Take logarithm:<br /><br />$\\ln y = \\tan^{-1} x \\cdot \\ln(x^2+1)$.<br /><br />Differentiate:<br /><br />$\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{1}{1+x^2}\\ln(x^2+1) + \\tan^{-1}x \\cdot \\frac{2x}{x^2+1}$.<br /><br />Thus,<br /><br />$\\frac{dy}{dx}=(x^2+1)^{\\tan^{-1}x}\\left(\\frac{2x\\tan^{-1}x}{x^2+1}+\\frac{1}{1+x^2}\\ln(x^2+1)\\right)$.",
  "explanation_image": [],
  "options": {
    "A": "$(x^2+1)^{\\tan^{-1}x}\\left(\\frac{2x}{x^2+1}+\\frac{\\ln(x^2+1)}{1+x^2}\\right)$",
    "B": "$(x^2+1)^{\\tan^{-1}x}\\left(\\frac{2x\\tan^{-1}x}{x^2+1}-\\frac{\\ln(x^2+1)}{1+x^2}\\right)$",
